<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Berkeley (governor)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1605">1605</a>–<a href="/years/1677">1677</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Berkeley was an English colonial governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, serving from 1641 to 1652 and again from 1660 to 1677. A royalist, he encouraged diversified agriculture but favoured a small circle of planters. His refusal to wage war on Native Americans on the frontier provoked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bacons-rebellion">Bacon's Rebellion</a> in 1676, which he suppressed with many executions before he was recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-berkeley-governor.jpg" alt="William Berkeley (governor)" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Painter Hariott L.T. Montague after Sir Peter Lely[1] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Governor_William_Berkeley_(grayscale).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Berkeley was born in 1605 in Bruton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, to Maurice Berkeley and Elizabeth Killigrew of the Bruton branch of the Berkeley family, both shareholders in the Virginia Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>; his elder brother was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-berkeley-1st-baron-berkeley-of-stratton">John Berkeley, 1st Baron Berkeley of Stratton</a>. His father died in debt when he was twelve. He studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rutgers-university">Queen's College</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, and then at St Edmund Hall. In 1632 he gained a place in the household of Charles I and joined a court literary circle known as the Wits. His play The Lost Lady was performed before the king and Henrietta Maria and published in 1638, and service in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishops-wars">Bishops' Wars</a> of 1639 and 1640 earned him a knighthood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Berkeley replaced Sir Francis Wyatt as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. He encouraged planters to grow more than tobacco, by law and by his own example: at Green Spring, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a>, he tried crops from wheat and barley to oranges and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a>, fruits, potash, silk and spirits to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and Holland, and grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> on the advice of enslaved people who knew it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. After the execution of Charles I in 1649 a parliamentary fleet forced him to give up his authority, though he was allowed to remain on his plantation as a private person. At the Restoration in 1660 he was reappointed.</p><p>In his second term he pursued a diverse economy, free trade, a close-knit colonial society and autonomy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> through the General Assembly. He was bitterly hostile to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a>, helped pass a law punishing ministers who preached outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, and opposed public education and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>. He oversaw the policy of partus sequitur ventrem, under which children born to enslaved parents took their mother's legal status, and Charles II made him one of the eight Lords Proprietors of Carolina.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1675 Berkeley gave high office to Nathaniel Bacon, his wife's nephew. Berkeley was slow to respond to Indian attacks on settlers, and disagreement over Indian policy led Bacon to command an illegal troop of volunteers. Berkeley declared him a rebel, but Bacon led 500 armed men into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> and forced the legislators to make him general. The upper hand returned to Berkeley before 1,000 soldiers sent by the crown arrived in January 1677. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, died at Berkeley House, Mayfair, on 9 July 1677, and was buried at St Mary's Church, Twickenham.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Berkeley (governor)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Governor <b>William Berkeley</b>'s rule provoked Bacon's Rebellion in 1676.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-virginia">Colony of Virginia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middlesex">Middlesex</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Mayfair, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.54,-0.27&amp;year=1651" aria-label="Middlesex on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of William Berkeley (governor)"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4986 5721h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4986 5721h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.54,-0.27&amp;year=1651">What was here in 1651? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
